      "text": "Solution: Let $A$ represent the event of picking 1 red candy, 1 blue candy, and 1 red candy, 1 blue candy (mixed red and blue), 1 red candy, 1 blue candy (mixed red and blue) or 1 candy each of red and blue candies.\n\nTo find $P(A)$, consider that Terry can select any two of the $20$ candies in ${20\\choose 2}$ ways. The same approach counts the ways Mary can pick any two of 10 candies. Hence, the probability of obtaining 1 candy each of same colors is\n\\[\\frac{(1 {1}\\choose 1 2{10}\\choose 1) + (1{7}\\choose 1 2{9} \\choose 1) + (1{6}\\choose 1 2{8} \\choose 1) + (1{5}\\choose\t1 2{7} \\choose 1) + (1{4}\\choose 1 2{6} \\choose 1) }{{20\\choose 2} {10\\choose 2}}\t= \\frac{3}{17},\\]\nand two of one color in 4 ${20 \\choose 2}$ ways that give 20 ${10 \\choose 2} - 4(1 {10 \\choose 1} 2{9} \\choose 1) $ as the number of favorable cases. Thus, $P(A) = 1 - \\frac{3}{1} = \\boxed{\\frac{17}{19}}$. The answer is \\frac{17}{19}",
