      "text": "First, consider the tree diagram. Variations of $RR$, $BB$, $RB$, and $BR$ are allowed. \n\nFor the case $RR$, Terry has $10 \\times 9$ ways to pick. For the next pick, there are $9$ white candies left, so Terry has $9$ choices. Mary has the same options, meaning the probability is $\\frac{1}{2}$.\nFor the case $BB$, Terry grabs $10 \\times 9$ \n\n$=$ $90$ ways, and then for Mary, there are $9 \\times 8$. Meaning Mary has $3$ more ways to pick, namely $$\\BB, \\ \\BB \\RR, \\ \\BB R$$. Again, she can pick either case, so the probability is $\\frac{1}{3}$.\nFor the case $RB$, Terry can grab any two reds in $10 \\times 9$ ways. Mary has similarly $18$ ways to choose.\nFor the case $BR$, Mary can pick any of $2 \\times 9$ ways, and then pick any of $18$ ($9$ reds then $9$ blues),$ $2 \\times 18$. Mary picks $2!$ ways to choose the order, for a total of $18 \\times 2$\nExtrapolating, we see regular repetition states that Terry and Mary have $135$ different options here, and a probability of $\\frac{3}{8}$ to guess to right, which some can figure out more quickly. This results in $$\\frac{1170}{2008},$$ as the denominator is the smaller. Thus, $m + n = \\boxed{3179}$. The answer is 3179",
